{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Find water adsorption isotherm data for aluminum, Al2O3, native oxide surfaces, and AlSi10Mg powders as a function of RH and temperature",
  "rationale": "Core to question 1 - need to establish whether RH or absolute humidity controls adsorption, and get quantitative isotherm data",
  "status": "in-progress",
  "result": "Found Deng 2008 data: on Al2O3, ~1 monolayer at 15% RH, 2 layers at 35-40% RH, 4 layers at 70% RH, 6 layers at 90% RH. Adsorption is parameterized by RH (water activity). Need more data on temperature effects and AlSi10Mg.",
  "evaluation": null
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Find water adsorption isotherm data for aluminum, Al2O3, native oxide surfaces, and AlSi10Mg powders",
  "rationale": "Core to question 1",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found: Deng 2008 Al2O3 isotherm (1 monolayer at 15% RH, 2 at 35-40%, 4 at 70%, 6 at 90%), Graedel 1989 Fig. 1 (aluminum surface: ~7-8 monolayers at 10% RH, ~12-13 at 30-50% RH, ~20+ at 95% RH). Adsorption is controlled by RH (water activity). SiO2 similar BET-type isotherm ~2 monolayers icelike + liquid above 60% RH.",
  "evaluation": "Good quantitative data from Deng and Graedel. Sufficient for answer."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 2, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 2,
  "objective": "Find water adsorption data for silicon surfaces/powders",
  "rationale": "The user asks about silicon powder storage",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Asay 2006 and Torun 2014 show SiO2 adsorbs ~2 monolayers of icelike water by ~60% RH, then liquid-like water above. Rapid initial uptake 0-20% RH. Generally similar BET-type isotherm as Al2O3.",
  "evaluation": "Sufficient qualitative data."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 3, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 3,
  "objective": "Find oxidation kinetics, rate laws, and activation energies for aluminum/Al-alloy powders at near-ambient conditions",
  "rationale": "Core to question 2",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Cabrera-Mott field-assisted model governs low-T oxidation; activation energies from literature: 71-150 kJ/mol range. At low T, field-driven rather than purely Arrhenius. AlSi10Mg oxide grows from ~4 nm (virgin) to 38 nm over 30 months reuse. Bayerite/pseudoboehmite form at 25°C, 90% RH. Critical RH for atmospheric corrosion ~70% RH for pristine Al (Graedel, Scher).",
  "evaluation": "Good data on Ea range, critical RH, and oxide growth rates."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 4, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 4,
  "objective": "Find information on critical RH thresholds",
  "rationale": "Question 2 specifically asks about critical RH",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "~70% RH is the experimentally observed threshold for atmospheric corrosion on pristine aluminum (Graedel 1989, Scher 2023). Below this, ion mobility is suppressed by >2 orders of magnitude. 30-40% RH should be well below critical threshold for active corrosion.",
  "evaluation": "Clear evidence from multiple sources."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 5, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 5,
  "objective": "Find data on alloying element effects on humidity sensitivity",
  "rationale": "Core to question 3",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Mg: surface enrichment in AlSi10Mg, forms MgAl2O4 spinel, promotes oxide growth. 7xxx Al-Zn-Mg-Cu: highly sensitive to H-EIC in humid air at 50% RH, high-Zn alloys ~10x faster crack growth vs older 7xxx. Li: extremely moisture-reactive, hydrolysis generates H2. Sc: forms Sc2O3 which can improve corrosion resistance. Ce/Er/Zr: limited specific powder data.",
  "evaluation": "Good data on Mg, Zn, and Li. Sc has some data. Ce/Er/Zr lack specific powder-humidity data."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 6, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 6,
  "objective": "Find data on stainless steel powder sensitivity",
  "rationale": "Question 3 asks about SS powder",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Kim 2025 shows 316L powder humidity sensitivity: increasing RH from 5-70% increases moisture, O and H content, and reduces flowability. But 316L has Cr2O3 passive layer - much more stable than Al. A 2-5°C change alone is insignificant for SS at near-ambient temperatures.",
  "evaluation": "Sufficient data."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 7, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
